 manage their  i think everyone's an adult but how they  could better manage their time towards  seeing that project through there's some  large  larger firms where like literally the  project managers will not share  libraries with one another  so if we had a better culture just  around share like knowledge sharing yes  we are competing with other architecture  firms but i think we're competing with  so much more out in the world if we play  our cars right and if we raise the value  of the architects then there's plenty of  work out there for all of us  so we should be helping each other out  more than we should be  worrying about who we're competing with  but how do we actually raise the value  of architects i mean from my experience  the general public already has a very  little understanding of what we actually  do and i mean we're already so  overworked  with tasks beyond just the design of the  building at least from my work at  mk think we  the strategy group in particular was  created because we felt that architects  were brought in too late  into  the the conversation and we didn't stay  around long enough what can we do from a  real estate and planning perspective to  help clients decide whether or not they  even need a building  we always talk about kind of adaptive  reuse  being key to sustainability right now  and we have such older buildings stock  and it's good to do that but like okay  so what type of services do we need to  provide to make sure that we're in front  of the client when we're they're  deciding  where like where their business needs to  be located how big their footprint needs  to be and what what type of building  they need to be in to be successful i  think there's there's a lot more on the  front end that frankly  i would our architects are going to  listen to this and say we already do  that we do that for free though  so often so those are services that we  should be  charging for i also have a lot of  friends  and younger  younger firms that have gone  into supporting like branding and design  development for businesses at a really  young stage and then when those  businesses need are ready to have brick  and mortar space  those architecture firms are the first  that they turn to  and then on on the back end i think  there's especially with all of the  information that is contained with our  in our bim models and all of the  modeling we do around how energy  efficient  our buildings are supposed to be i think  there's a little big opportunity to look  at can we move to  at least outside the realm of  like residential  operators of space and can we help our  help the new owners better operate their  spaces going forward so they function  as efficiently as we intended them to  that's i mean that's just two two very  high level ways that we could look at um  expanding our services beyond the life  of the  not beyond the life of the building  we're actually expanding our services to  include the entire life of the building  rather than  just the part where we're improving the  building right  yeah yeah a lot of times i feel like we  have a lot of that information yeah like  in-house  but we just have to spend the time to  filter it and make it usable for people  and make people understand it in order  for us to do that  we need to have  flexibility and like fees and  time  so yeah it's kind of a chicken or the  egg so  i think the theme here is thinking  beyond just construction  and i think it makes a lot of sense like  whether you're designing a library or a  restaurant the design of the building  itself is actually kind of a small part  of the process and frankly  like stakeholders and business owners  they really care about the operations of  the space rather than the look or the  design of the space but at the same time  when you're running a project and you're  working at the firm  we rarely have time to build new skill  sets let alone  build an entirely new area of expertise  so how can a