periences means that you can be a  contributor from day one and i thought  that was like  a really  motivating thing for like  young people who just like started a  firm that's the line that we draw it  doesn't matter if you're an intern it  doesn't matter if you're coming in as a  senior vice president like you made it  through our hiring like process  if it was a rigorous and robust enough  hiring process then we believe that you  have valuable contributions to make to  this organization  and you should question you should have  the ability to question everything that  we're doing and if we don't have an  answer we should be questioning if we're  doing it the right way we're not going  to invite you to the client meeting  until x y and z you aren't going to come  out onto the construction site until you  pass all these things i think other  industries kind of  and and this is not true of all firms i  realize i'm making general  generalizations but i feel like the  transparency of the communication kind  of throwing people into the middle of  things happens  on a more frequent basis  outside of architecture i think the  businesses that are going to thrive in  the future are the ones that are gonna  leverage their human connections and  build not just spaces for people but  build business models that focuses on  people but so there's this really small  practice i would say of like five people  or less in la  um just this season called stainer  architects  and they have this love of food and the  the guy there like started up this  relationship with this friend who has  deep expertise in culinary work so  they're this architecture firm that also  does a lot with food systems and they  actually help  restaurants  with menu planning and their entire food  systems and then they've designed and  developed and deliver and own a few of  their own they own a wine bar they own a  restaurant they own a few of their own  hospitality spaces so that's just kind  of like  i mean 